// Fresh Question Bank - Domain 4.0 Security Operations (part B: 4.5-4.9)
window.FRESH_BANK = (window.FRESH_BANK || []).concat([
// ---------- 4.5 Modify enterprise capabilities (26) ----------
{o:"4.5",q:"Jordan needs to publish, in DNS, the list of IP addresses and hosts permitted to send mail for his company's domain, so that receiving servers can compare the connecting IP of each message against it. Which record should he create?",c:["An SPF TXT record listing the authorized sending hosts","A DMARC TXT record published at the _dmarc label in DNS","A DKIM TXT record holding a selector public key","An MX record naming the inbound mail exchangers"],a:0,e:["Correct. SPF is a TXT record that enumerates the hosts and IP ranges allowed to send for the domain, and the receiver checks the connecting IP against it. That matches the requirement to list permitted senders.","Incorrect. DMARC publishes a policy (none, quarantine, reject) and a reporting address, and it relies on SPF and DKIM results. It does not itself list sending hosts. It would be right when the goal is to tell receivers what to do with failures.","Incorrect. A DKIM record publishes a public key so receivers can verify a message signature. It says nothing about which IPs may send. It would be right if the stem asked for cryptographic proof that content was unaltered.","Incorrect. MX records tell senders where to deliver inbound mail for the domain, which is the opposite direction from authorizing outbound senders. They would be right when configuring where the company receives mail."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"A company wants each outbound message to carry a domain-level cryptographic signature that recipients can validate with a public key retrieved from DNS, proving the headers and body were not modified in transit. Which technology provides this?",c:["SPF alignment with the envelope sender address","S/MIME certificates issued to each individual mailbox","STARTTLS between the mail transfer agents on port 25","DKIM signing with a selector record published in DNS"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. SPF only checks whether the connecting IP is authorized for the envelope domain, and it provides no signature or integrity protection. It would be right when the question is about authorized sending servers.","Incorrect. S/MIME signs with a per-user certificate chained to a CA, not a domain key looked up in DNS. It would be the better answer if individual users needed non-repudiation or end-to-end encrypted mail.","Incorrect. STARTTLS encrypts the hop between two mail servers but does not sign the message, and the content is exposed again at each relay. It would be right if the requirement were transport confidentiality instead of message integrity.","Correct. DKIM signs selected headers and the body with the domain's private key, and receivers fetch the public key from the selector record in DNS to verify integrity. That is exactly a domain-level signature validated through DNS."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"A company has already deployed SPF and DKIM. Management now wants receiving servers to reject messages that fail those checks for the company domain, and wants daily aggregate reports about who is sending mail using the domain. What should be added?",c:["A second SPF TXT record that lists all additional senders","A DNSSEC chain of trust signed across the entire company zone","A DMARC record at _dmarc with p=reject and an rua address","A BIMI record publishing the verified company logo image"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A domain may publish only one SPF record, since multiples cause a permerror, and SPF has no reporting or policy-enforcement tag. Additional senders are added by merging them into the existing record.","Incorrect. DNSSEC authenticates DNS answers and prevents tampering with records, but it does not tell mail receivers how to treat failing messages or generate reports. It would be right for defending against cache poisoning.","Correct. DMARC ties the From domain to SPF and DKIM results, instructs receivers to reject failures through p=reject, and the rua tag requests aggregate reports. Both stated needs, enforcement and reporting, are met.","Incorrect. BIMI displays a verified brand logo in supporting mail clients and actually requires an enforced DMARC policy first. It is a presentation feature, not the enforcement and reporting mechanism requested here."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"Phishing emails keep getting through because a link in the message points to a clean site at delivery time and is redirected to a credential harvester hours later. Which email gateway capability BEST addresses this?",c:["Attachment sandboxing with detonation before delivery","URL rewriting with time-of-click reputation checks","Sender reputation scoring on the connecting IP address","Outbound DLP scanning of messages sent by employees"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Sandboxing detonates attachments and files to observe malicious behavior before delivery, but this threat is a hyperlink that changes later. It would be right for a weaponized document or archive.","Correct. Rewriting links so every click is re-evaluated catches destinations that turn malicious after delivery. The stem's key clue is that the link was clean when scanned and changed later.","Incorrect. IP reputation filters mail from known-bad senders at connection time, but it cannot see what a link will point to hours later. It would be right for blocking mass spam from listed sources.","Incorrect. Outbound DLP inspects mail leaving the company for sensitive data. It does not evaluate inbound links. It would be right if the concern were employees emailing customer records externally."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"A security architect must justify purchasing a secure email gateway to the board. The gateway will sit in front of the mail servers. Which statement BEST describes the value it adds?",c:["It authenticates each user to the mail portal by requiring a second factor before the mailbox opens","It inspects inbound mail for spam, malware and malicious links and enforces policy before delivery","It encrypts stored mailboxes on the server so a stolen disk cannot expose the messages inside","It restricts which hosts may relay through the organization's mail server based on source address"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Requiring a second factor is an identity control provided by the identity provider or the mail platform, not by a filtering gateway. It would be right for protecting against stolen passwords.","Correct. A secure email gateway filters at the mail perimeter using antispam, antivirus, sandboxing, URL checks and policy rules, which reduces what reaches users. That is the value described for a device placed in front of the mail servers.","Incorrect. Encryption of stored mailboxes is data-at-rest protection on the mail server or its storage. A gateway does not provide it. It would be right if the risk were physical theft of server disks.","Incorrect. Restricting open relay is a mail server configuration, usually done with relay restrictions and SPF-style allow lists. It limits who sends through the server, but it does not inspect content for threats."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"Auditors require the security team to be alerted whenever executables or configuration files on production servers change outside an approved change window. Which capability meets this requirement?",c:["File integrity monitoring comparing hashes to a baseline","Data loss prevention rules matching sensitive content patterns","Network access control checking posture on connecting hosts","Web filtering based on category and reputation databases"],a:0,e:["Correct. FIM records a known-good baseline of file hashes and attributes and alerts when files change, which maps directly to detecting unauthorized modification of system and configuration files. PCI DSS explicitly calls for it.","Incorrect. DLP looks for sensitive data moving through channels such as email, USB or uploads. It does not baseline system files. It would be right if the concern were customer records leaving the network.","Incorrect. NAC decides whether a device may join the network based on identity and posture. It does not watch file changes on servers already connected. It would be right for keeping noncompliant laptops off the LAN.","Incorrect. Web filtering controls which sites users can browse. It has no view of local file modifications. It would be right if the goal were blocking gambling or malware-hosting categories."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"The CISO wants to stop employees from sending customer records out of the company through personal webmail and from copying them to removable media. Which single deployment covers BOTH channels?",c:["FIM watching the file shares that hold the records","CASB inline proxy for sanctioned SaaS applications","Email gateway with attachment sandboxing enabled","DLP with network, email and endpoint components"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. FIM detects changes to files, not copies or transfers of them, so it would not stop data being read and sent out. It would be right for spotting tampering with the records.","Incorrect. A CASB governs cloud application usage and data in SaaS, and it would not control writing to removable media on a laptop. It would be right if the concern were uploads to unsanctioned cloud storage only.","Incorrect. A gateway with sandboxing protects against inbound malicious attachments and covers only the corporate email channel. It would not catch personal webmail or USB copies.","Correct. DLP policies applied at the network and email layer plus endpoint agents can block uploads to webmail and writes to USB storage, covering both channels named in the stem."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"An analyst wants a tool that learns what normal looks like for each account, such as typical logon hours and download volume, and then raises an alert when a user suddenly pulls 40 GB of files at 3 a.m. Which technology is described?",c:["Signature-based intrusion detection","Port security with sticky MAC addresses","User and entity behavior analytics","A web application firewall in blocking mode"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Signature-based IDS matches traffic against known attack patterns, and unusual but legitimate-looking file access has no signature. It would be right for detecting a known exploit.","Incorrect. Port security limits which MAC addresses can use a switch port. It does not analyze account behavior. It would be right for preventing an unauthorized device being plugged in.","Correct. UEBA builds behavioral baselines for users and entities and flags deviations, which is how insider threats and compromised accounts are surfaced. The stem describes baselining then alerting on an anomaly.","Incorrect. A WAF filters HTTP requests aimed at a web application, such as injection attempts. It does not baseline user behavior across systems. It would be right to protect a public web portal."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"A hospital wants to block connections to known malicious domains from every device on its network, including infusion pumps and cameras on which no software can be installed. Which approach fits BEST?",c:["An agent-based web filter on each managed laptop","A host-based firewall with outbound deny rules","DNS filtering at the company's recursive resolver","Endpoint antivirus with cloud-based reputation lookups"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A web filter agent protects only devices that can run it, which excludes pumps and cameras. It would be right for roaming laptops where per-device enforcement travels with the user.","Incorrect. A host firewall is software on each device, so it faces the same agent limitation, and it is difficult to maintain a list of malicious domains in rules. It would be right for restricting a server's ports.","Correct. Filtering at the resolver covers any device that uses the company DNS, with nothing installed on the endpoints. Devices that cannot run agents are the deciding clue.","Incorrect. Antivirus is installed on each endpoint, so appliances and cameras remain unprotected. It would be right for detecting malware on managed workstations."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"Sales staff work from hotels and home offices and only occasionally connect to the VPN. The company needs web policy enforced on their laptops regardless of the network they use. Which approach is BEST?",c:["A forward proxy located in the headquarters data center","An agent-based web filter installed on each laptop","An ACL on the headquarters edge router","A URL deny list on the headquarters firewall"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A proxy at headquarters only sees traffic that is sent to it, and these users are not on the VPN. It would be right when all users are on the office network or always-on VPN.","Correct. An agent on the laptop applies policy locally or through a cloud service wherever the device is connected, which is the requirement for staff who rarely use the VPN.","Incorrect. A router ACL filters by IP and port at the HQ edge, and home or hotel traffic never crosses it. It would be right for controlling traffic between HQ subnets.","Incorrect. A firewall deny list only applies to traffic passing the HQ firewall. Remote users browsing from hotel networks bypass it. It would be right for office-based staff."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"A web filter blocks gambling, adult content and newly registered domains using a vendor-maintained database that is updated continuously. Which feature does this describe?",c:["Content categorization with reputation scoring","SSL decryption with certificate pinning exceptions","Host-based allow listing of approved applications","Port mirroring to an intrusion detection sensor"],a:0,e:["Correct. Vendor categories and reputation scores let a filter allow or block entire classes of sites without manual lists. The continuously updated vendor database is the clue.","Incorrect. SSL decryption lets the filter inspect encrypted traffic, but it is not the category database that decides what to block. It would be right when HTTPS traffic must be examined.","Incorrect. Application allow listing controls which executables may run on a host, not which websites can be reached. It would be right for preventing unapproved software.","Incorrect. Port mirroring copies frames to a monitoring device and does not block anything by itself. It would be right for feeding a passive IDS or packet capture tool."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"A newly deployed IPS has signatures for the malware family but keeps missing attacks where the attacker recompiles the payload so its hash and byte patterns differ slightly each time. Which change BEST improves detection?",c:["Update the signature set to a more frequent schedule","Place the sensor in passive mode on a SPAN port","Increase the log retention period on the sensor","Add behavior-based detection alongside signatures"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Faster updates help with newly published signatures, but a payload that changes each time can evade any exact pattern. Updates are right for known threats the sensor has not yet learned.","Incorrect. Moving to passive mode removes the ability to block and does not improve detection logic. It would be right when testing an IDS without risk of blocking production traffic.","Incorrect. Longer retention helps later investigation, but it has no effect on whether an attack is detected at the time. It would be right to meet a compliance requirement for log history.","Correct. Behavior and anomaly detection looks at what traffic or code does rather than exact patterns, so it can catch polymorphic variants that evade signatures. The slightly modified payload is the clue."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"A Linux administrator needs mandatory access control that confines the web server process so it can read only its own content directory, even if the process is compromised and running as its normal service account. Which should be enabled?",c:["A chroot jail configured for the service directory","Sudoers rules limiting the service account's commands","Cron-based permission resets on the content folder","SELinux in enforcing mode with a targeted policy"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. chroot changes the apparent root directory, but it is not a MAC system and a root process can often escape it. It would be right for lightweight isolation of a legacy daemon.","Incorrect. sudoers rules control which commands users may run with elevated rights. They do not confine the process after compromise. They would be right for delegating specific administrative tasks.","Incorrect. Resetting permissions on a schedule is a reactive cleanup that leaves gaps between runs and does not restrict what the process itself can access. It would be right only as a housekeeping task.","Correct. SELinux enforces labels and policy on processes independent of file ownership, so a compromised web server process is confined to what the policy allows. The MAC requirement points to it."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"An administrator must push the same password policy, USB storage restrictions and firewall rules to 800 domain-joined Windows computers from one place. Which feature should be used?",c:["SELinux policy modules deployed to each host","SNMPv3 traps sent to a central network management station","Group Policy objects linked to organizational units","NetFlow exporters configured on the core switches"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. SELinux is a Linux mandatory access control framework and does not manage Windows. It would be right for hardening Red Hat servers.","Incorrect. SNMPv3 traps carry monitoring events from devices to a manager. They do not push configuration to Windows hosts. They would be right for alerting on network device conditions.","Correct. Group Policy centrally applies security settings to domain computers and users based on OU membership. Domain-joined Windows plus central enforcement is the clue.","Incorrect. NetFlow exports traffic statistics, so it is a visibility feature. It does not change host configuration. It would be right to analyze bandwidth use and conversations."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"The last entry in the perimeter firewall rule set is a deny-all rule that matches any source, destination and port. What is the purpose of this rule?",c:["Apply stateful inspection to established sessions","Implement implicit deny for unlisted traffic","Enable NAT translation for outbound connections","Create a screened subnet for public servers"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Stateful inspection tracks connection state and allows return traffic, which is separate from the catch-all rule. It would be right when explaining how reply packets are permitted automatically.","Correct. A final deny any/any makes the default behavior explicit: anything not matched by an earlier allow rule is blocked, which follows the principle of least privilege.","Incorrect. NAT translates addresses between internal and external networks, and it is not configured by a deny rule. It would be right when hiding private addressing behind a public IP.","Incorrect. A screened subnet is a network design with separate segments for public-facing servers, which is not created by one rule. It would be right when isolating web servers from the internal LAN."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"A mail relay must send outgoing messages to a partner's server using SMTP over implicit TLS, where the connection is encrypted from the first byte. Which port rule must be allowed?",c:["TCP 465 from the relay to the partner mail server","TCP 587 from the relay to the partner mail server","TCP 143 from the relay to the partner mail server","TCP 995 from the relay to the partner mail server"],a:0,e:["Correct. SMTPS with implicit TLS uses TCP 465, with the session encrypted from the start. The phrase from the first byte distinguishes it from STARTTLS.","Incorrect. TCP 587 is message submission, which normally upgrades to TLS with STARTTLS after connecting. It would be right if the stem described explicit TLS negotiation by clients.","Incorrect. TCP 143 is IMAP for mailbox access, not for sending mail. IMAP over implicit TLS uses 993. It would be right for configuring clients to read mail.","Incorrect. TCP 995 is POP3 over implicit TLS used to retrieve mail. It is a client retrieval protocol and not used by a relay for sending."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"The firewall team must allow a site-to-site IPsec VPN to a partner. The peer devices sit behind NAT. Which rules are required for tunnel establishment?",c:["UDP 500 and UDP 4500 for IKE and NAT traversal","TCP 22 and TCP 443 for management and web access","UDP 123 and UDP 514 for time sync and logging","TCP 1701 and UDP 1723 for L2TP and PPTP control"],a:0,e:["Correct. IKE negotiates on UDP 500, and UDP 4500 carries IKE and ESP when NAT traversal is needed. ESP also has to pass where there is no NAT. Behind NAT is the clue.","Incorrect. TCP 22 is SSH and TCP 443 is HTTPS. These support administration or an SSL VPN, but they do not establish IPsec. They would be right for a clientless web portal.","Incorrect. UDP 123 is NTP and UDP 514 is syslog. They support timekeeping and logging, not tunnel setup. They would be right when permitting infrastructure services.","Incorrect. These are older tunneling protocols, with L2TP control on UDP 1701 and PPTP on TCP 1723, so the protocol numbers listed are mismatched. PPTP is deprecated. They would be right for a legacy remote access VPN."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"A server must forward its logs to the SIEM over an encrypted syslog channel. A firewall change request needs the correct port and protocol. Which one?",c:["UDP 514 from the server to the SIEM collector","UDP 162 from the server to the SIEM collector","UDP 1812 from the server to the SIEM collector","TCP 6514 from the server to the SIEM collector"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. UDP 514 is traditional syslog, which is unencrypted and unreliable. It would be right if the stem allowed clear-text, best-effort delivery.","Incorrect. UDP 162 receives SNMP traps from network devices. It does not carry syslog. It would be right for forwarding trap messages to a monitoring station.","Incorrect. UDP 1812 is the RADIUS authentication port and is not used for syslog. It would be right for allowing authentication requests to a RADIUS server.","Correct. Syslog over TLS is standardized on TCP 6514, and encryption requires a connection-oriented transport. The encrypted requirement is the discriminating clue."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"The application tier needs access to a Microsoft SQL Server instance in the database tier using its default listening port. Which rule should be added?",c:["TCP 1521 from the application servers to the database servers","TCP 3306 from the application tier servers to the database servers","TCP 1433 from the application servers to the database servers","TCP 5432 from the application servers to the database servers"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. TCP 1521 is the default listener for Oracle Database. It would be right if the back end were an Oracle server.","Incorrect. TCP 3306 is the MySQL and MariaDB default. It would be right for an open-source MySQL back end.","Correct. Microsoft SQL Server listens on TCP 1433 by default, and the rule direction matches app tier to database tier.","Incorrect. TCP 5432 is PostgreSQL's default port. It would be right when the database tier runs PostgreSQL."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"A firewall rule must block guests on the Wi-Fi network from pinging the server subnet while leaving other traffic rules unchanged. How should the protocol and port fields be set?",c:["Protocol TCP, port 7 for the echo service","Protocol ICMP, port field not applicable","Protocol UDP, port 161 for SNMP queries","Protocol TCP, port 80 for HTTP requests"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. TCP 7 is the legacy echo service and is not what ping uses. It would be right only when blocking that obsolete service.","Correct. Ping uses ICMP echo request and reply messages, and ICMP operates at the network layer without ports, so the rule matches on protocol and type.","Incorrect. UDP 161 is used for SNMP polling, which does not involve ICMP echo. It would be right for restricting management queries to network devices.","Incorrect. TCP 80 is plain HTTP web traffic and would leave ping unaffected while breaking web access. It would be right for controlling web access."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"The company wants to discover which unsanctioned SaaS applications employees use and to apply data upload policies to the sanctioned ones, using logs, APIs and inline enforcement. Which solution is designed for this?",c:["A network access control server","A cloud access security broker","A web application firewall","A security information and event manager"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. NAC controls whether devices may join the network based on identity and posture. It does not govern use of external SaaS services. It would be right for admitting only compliant devices.","Correct. A CASB provides visibility into shadow IT and enforces policies such as DLP and access control for cloud applications, using API and inline modes. That is the described need.","Incorrect. A WAF protects a specific web application you host from attacks like injection. It does not inventory employee SaaS use. It would be right for securing the company's own site.","Incorrect. A SIEM correlates logs and alerts but does not enforce policy on cloud app usage by itself. It would be right for central log analysis and detection."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"Attackers have been spoofing the CEO's address to send fake payment requests to the company's outside partners. Which control, applied to the company's own domain, BEST stops partner mail servers from accepting those forgeries?",c:["A DMARC reject policy with aligned SPF and DKIM","An internal spam filter tuned for executive names","A banner added to inbound messages by the gateway","A mailbox rule forwarding wire requests to finance"],a:0,e:["Correct. External receivers retrieve the sender domain's DMARC policy from DNS and reject unauthenticated mail claiming that domain. It works for outside partners because the check happens on their side.","Incorrect. An internal filter protects only the company's own mailboxes and cannot influence how partners' servers handle mail from the spoofed domain. It would be right for protecting internal staff.","Incorrect. An inbound warning banner is applied to the company's incoming messages and does not help partners at all. It would be right to warn staff about external senders.","Incorrect. A forwarding rule does not stop forged mail from reaching partners, and it just reroutes company mail. Process controls like callback verification would be right to reduce financial fraud."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"A change ticket reads: Allow the proxy server at 10.10.10.30 to make outbound HTTP requests to the Internet on behalf of users. Which firewall rule implements this correctly?",c:["Source Internet, destination 10.10.10.30, TCP 80, allow","Source 10.10.10.30, destination Internet, UDP 80, allow","Source 10.10.10.0/24, destination Internet, TCP 80, allow","Source 10.10.10.30, destination Internet, TCP 80, allow"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. This reverses the direction, permitting the Internet to initiate connections to the proxy. It would be right to publish a service hosted on that server.","Incorrect. HTTP runs over TCP, and UDP 80 is not what a proxy uses for web requests. UDP would apply to protocols like DNS or QUIC on other ports.","Incorrect. The /24 would permit every host on the subnet to go straight out, which exceeds the ticket's scope of one proxy. It would be right if the request covered the whole subnet.","Correct. The proxy initiates the connections, so it is the source, the destination is the Internet, and HTTP uses TCP 80. The rule is limited to the single host named in the ticket."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"A vendor platform collects telemetry from endpoints, network and cloud sources and can automatically isolate a host and kill a malicious process after correlating events. Which category is described?",c:["A cloud secure web gateway (SWG) service","A network access control (NAC) policy server","An extended detection and response platform","A unified threat management (UTM) appliance"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A secure web gateway filters web traffic for users. It does not collect endpoint telemetry or kill processes. It would be right for enforcing browsing policy.","Incorrect. NAC admits or quarantines devices based on policy at connection time and does not correlate telemetry from several sources. It would be right for gating access to the LAN.","Correct. XDR extends EDR across multiple telemetry sources and supports automated containment actions such as host isolation and process termination. Cross-domain correlation plus response is the clue.","Incorrect. UTM bundles firewall, IPS, AV and filtering into one network appliance. It lacks endpoint-level response. It would be right for a small office wanting consolidated perimeter security."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"Two Windows file servers must exchange files using SMB without the legacy NetBIOS session service. Which port should the firewall allow between them?",c:["TCP 3389 between the two file servers","TCP 21 between the two file servers","TCP 445 between the two file servers","UDP 137 between the two file servers"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. TCP 3389 is Remote Desktop Protocol, which provides remote GUI sessions, not file sharing. It would be right for allowing administrators to connect to servers remotely.","Incorrect. TCP 21 is the FTP control channel. It would be right for an FTP transfer, not for Windows file sharing.","Correct. Modern SMB runs directly over TCP 445, without NetBIOS. The stem's exclusion of NetBIOS is the clue.","Incorrect. UDP 137 is the NetBIOS name service used by legacy SMB. It would be right for supporting very old clients that rely on NetBIOS name resolution."]},
{o:"4.5",q:"A security team wants resolvers to verify that DNS answers were signed by the zone owner so that forged records injected into a cache are rejected. What should they implement?",c:["DMARC with a reject policy for the domain","DNSSEC signing with validating resolvers","DHCP snooping on the access-layer switches","DoH on the user browsers to the resolver"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. DMARC governs how mail receivers handle messages that fail authentication and does not protect DNS answers. It would be right for countering email spoofing.","Correct. DNSSEC signs records and lets validating resolvers check authenticity and integrity, which defeats cache poisoning and forged answers. The signed-by-owner clue points to it.","Incorrect. DHCP snooping blocks rogue DHCP servers on switches. It would be right if the issue were clients receiving bad leases, not DNS record forgery.","Incorrect. DNS over HTTPS encrypts the query path between client and resolver, which protects privacy, but it does not prove the answer was signed by the zone owner. It would be right to hide queries from eavesdroppers."]},

// ---------- 4.6 Identity and access management (26) ----------
{o:"4.6",q:"Employees sign in once at the corporate portal and then open Salesforce, Workday and Slack without re-entering credentials. The identity provider passes signed XML assertions to each application. Which protocol is in use?",c:["SAML 2.0 between the IdP and service providers","OAuth 2.0 authorization code flow with scopes","LDAP simple bind against the directory server","RADIUS authentication through a network access server"],a:0,e:["Correct. SAML federates authentication by having the IdP issue signed XML assertions that service providers trust, which is the enterprise web SSO pattern described. The XML assertions are the clue.","Incorrect. OAuth 2.0 issues access tokens for delegated authorization to APIs, and it does not define XML identity assertions. It would be right when an app needs limited access to a user's data on another service.","Incorrect. LDAP is a directory access protocol used to query or bind to a directory, and each application would still collect the password. It would be right for a lookup of users and groups.","Incorrect. RADIUS provides AAA for network access such as Wi-Fi, VPN or switches, not web application SSO. It would be right for authenticating wireless users with 802.1X."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"A fitness app asks to read the user's Google Calendar. The user must approve a limited permission and the app must never see the user's Google password. Which protocol enables this?",c:["SAML 2.0 with a signed assertion from an IdP","Kerberos with a service ticket from the KDC","TACACS+ with per-command authorization","OAuth 2.0 with a consented scope and token"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. SAML authenticates users to applications for SSO and does not grant scoped API access to another app. It would be right for a workforce SSO to a SaaS tool.","Incorrect. Kerberos issues tickets inside a realm or domain for mutual authentication. It is not designed for third-party apps on the web. It would be right for domain-joined internal services.","Incorrect. TACACS+ authenticates and authorizes administrators on network devices, with per-command control. It would be right for restricting what an engineer can type on a router.","Correct. OAuth 2.0 lets a user grant a third-party app scoped access through a token without sharing credentials. The delegated, limited permission is the clue."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"A developer wants Sign in with Google on a new web app. The app needs to learn who the user is, receiving an ID token in JWT format, rather than just obtaining API access. Which protocol should be used?",c:["Plain OAuth 2.0 with an access token only","SAML 2.0 with an encrypted XML assertion","LDAPS with a service account bind","OpenID Connect built on top of OAuth 2.0"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. An OAuth access token represents authorization to call an API, not a standardized proof of who the user is. It would be right when only delegated access to resources is needed.","Incorrect. SAML can authenticate users but uses XML assertions and is typically used for enterprise SSO rather than consumer sign-in for modern web and mobile apps. It would be right in an enterprise federation.","Incorrect. LDAPS is a secured directory protocol, and using it would make the app handle user passwords directly. It would be right when an internal application must query a corporate directory.","Correct. OIDC adds an identity layer to OAuth 2.0, returning an ID token (JWT) that tells the app who authenticated. Needing the user's identity is the clue."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"Two companies in a joint venture agree that each company's identity provider will be trusted by the other, so partner employees can access shared applications using their own home credentials. What is this arrangement?",c:["Attestation of entitlements by managers","Provisioning through an HR-driven workflow","Federation between identity providers","Single sign-on within one security domain"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Attestation is a periodic review where owners confirm that users should keep their access. It would be right for an access recertification campaign.","Incorrect. Provisioning creates and modifies accounts and entitlements. It does not create trust between organizations. It would be right for automating onboarding of new employees.","Correct. Federation establishes trust between separate identity providers so users authenticate at home and are accepted elsewhere. Separate organizations trusting each other is the clue.","Incorrect. SSO inside one domain lets users authenticate once to many apps owned by the same organization, with no cross-organization trust. It would be right for internal applications under one IdP."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"A user signs in with a password and then taps Approve on a push notification sent to an enrolled phone. Which pair of authentication factors does this use?",c:["Something you are plus something you know","Something you know and something you have","Something you have and somewhere you are","Something you do and something you are"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. No fingerprint, face or other inherent trait is evaluated in this flow. This pairing would apply if the user also scanned a fingerprint after typing the password.","Correct. The password is knowledge and the enrolled phone that receives the approval is possession.","Incorrect. The password is not possession, and the push approval is not based on geographic location. This pairing would apply to a smart card combined with an IP or GPS restriction.","Incorrect. Neither a behavioral pattern, such as a gait or typing rhythm, nor a biometric trait is checked. This pairing would apply to behavioral biometrics combined with a fingerprint."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"An auditor says the login for a legacy system is still single-factor even though users must provide two items. Which combination explains the auditor's finding?",c:["A password plus a PIN code","A password plus a fingerprint","A smart card plus a PIN code","A password plus a hardware token"],a:0,e:["Correct. Both a password and a PIN are something you know, so they are the same factor type, and the login remains single-factor.","Incorrect. A password is knowledge and a fingerprint is inherence, so these are two different factor types. This is genuine multifactor authentication.","Incorrect. A smart card is possession and a PIN is knowledge, so two factor types are used. This is genuine multifactor authentication.","Incorrect. A password is knowledge and a hardware token is possession, so two factor types are used. This is genuine multifactor authentication."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"A bank allows online banking only when the login attempt originates from a device located inside the customer's home country, as determined by GPS or IP geolocation. Which factor category does this represent?",c:["The somewhere you are factor","The something you know factor","The something you are factor","The something you do factor"],a:0,e:["Correct. Location-based checks such as geofencing or geolocation are the somewhere-you-are factor, commonly used as a supplemental condition.","Incorrect. Something you know is a secret such as a password or PIN, and the user does not enter anything here. It would be right for a security-question prompt.","Incorrect. Something you are refers to biometric traits such as fingerprint or iris. The country the device is in is not part of the person. It would be right for a facial scan.","Incorrect. Something you do refers to behavior such as a signature style or typing rhythm. Location is not a behavior pattern. It would be right for keystroke dynamics."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"A company wants passwordless logins that use a public-private key pair kept on the user's device, unlocked locally with a biometric or PIN, and bound to the website so it cannot be replayed on a phishing site. Which method fits?",c:["SMS one-time passwords delivered to a phone number","Knowledge-based answers to security questions","HMAC-based one-time passwords from a counter token","Passkeys based on FIDO2 and WebAuthn"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. SMS codes are shared secrets sent over the carrier network, and they can be intercepted or phished. They would be right only as a weak fallback second factor.","Incorrect. Security questions are weak knowledge factors that attackers can research or guess. They would be right only for legacy account recovery.","Incorrect. HOTP creates a code from a shared secret and a counter and is not public-key based, so a phishing site can capture and replay it. It would be right for a hardware token using counter-based codes.","Correct. Passkeys use asymmetric keys and origin binding, so authentication fails on a lookalike site, which provides phishing resistance. Key pair plus origin binding is the clue."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"An authenticator app displays a six-digit code that is regenerated every 30 seconds using a secret shared at enrollment and the current clock. Which standard does it implement?",c:["HOTP as specified in RFC 4226","CHAP as specified in RFC 1994","TOTP as specified in RFC 6238","NTLM as specified in MS-NLMP"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. HOTP derives codes from a shared secret and an incrementing counter, so a code remains valid until used. It would be right for a hardware token that generates a code on each button press.","Incorrect. CHAP is a challenge-response protocol used for PPP authentication with a hashed challenge. It does not produce codes on a time schedule. It would be right for a legacy dial-up link.","Correct. TOTP derives each code from a shared secret and the current time step, which matches a code that refreshes every 30 seconds.","Incorrect. NTLM is a Windows challenge-response authentication protocol, not a one-time code generator. It would be relevant for legacy Windows network authentication."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"Hospital permissions are granted by job function. Anyone assigned the Nurse job gets chart read access, and a user who moves to Billing Clerk loses that access and gets the billing permissions instead. Which model is this?",c:["Mandatory access control (MAC)","Role-based access control (RBAC)","Discretionary access control (DAC)","Rule-based access control (RuBAC)"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. MAC compares system-enforced labels and clearances, such as Secret or Top Secret. It would be right for classified government data.","Correct. RBAC assigns permissions to roles that match job functions, and users receive access by role membership. Moving jobs means changing roles.","Incorrect. DAC lets a resource owner decide who may access it. Here the job function, not the owner, determines access. It would be right for NTFS folders shared by their creator.","Incorrect. Rule-based access control evaluates conditions such as time of day or source address that apply to everyone. It would be right if access depended on those rules rather than the job."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"On a shared project drive, the person who created each folder decides which colleagues may read or edit it, and can later pass that decision to someone else. Which access control model is this?",c:["Role-based access control (RBAC)","Discretionary access control (DAC)","Mandatory access control (MAC)","Attribute-based access control (ABAC)"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. RBAC grants access through roles defined by administrators rather than by individual owners. It would be right if access followed job titles.","Correct. In DAC the owner of the resource has discretion over who can access it and can delegate that control, as with NTFS permissions set by a folder owner.","Incorrect. MAC removes discretion from users, since the system enforces labels and clearances. It would be right where users cannot share data beyond classification.","Incorrect. ABAC evaluates policies against attributes of the user, resource and environment. It would be right when decisions combine several attributes rather than owner choice."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"A defense contractor labels files Confidential, Secret or Top Secret. The operating system allows a read only when the user's clearance is at least the file's label, and users cannot change labels or share around the rule. Which model is this?",c:["Mandatory access control","Discretionary access control","Role-based access control","Rule-based access control"],a:0,e:["Correct. MAC uses system-enforced labels and clearances that owners and users cannot override. Labels plus clearance with no user discretion is the clue.","Incorrect. DAC would let the file's owner decide who may read it, contradicting the requirement that users cannot share around the rule. It would be right for ordinary shared folders.","Incorrect. RBAC assigns permissions according to job roles and does not compare classification labels with clearance levels. It would be right for a hospital with defined job functions.","Incorrect. Rule-based control applies conditions like source address or time window for everyone. It would be right for limiting access to business hours, not classification levels."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"A policy grants access to the quarterly financials only if the user is in the Finance department AND is on a company-managed device AND the request is during business hours. Which model evaluates this combination of properties?",c:["Discretionary access control (DAC)","Mandatory access control (MAC)","Role-based access control (RBAC) alone","Attribute-based access control (ABAC)"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. DAC leaves the decision to the data owner and is not evaluated against departmental, device and time attributes. It would be right when the owner shares a folder at will.","Incorrect. MAC compares classification labels with clearances and has no notion of device management status or business hours. It would be right for a labeled military data system.","Incorrect. RBAC alone maps users to permissions through roles and cannot express device state and time conditions without extra rules. It would be right if department membership were the only requirement.","Correct. ABAC makes decisions from attributes of the user (department), the device or environment (managed, time) and the resource, so it can express this combined policy."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"The HR system must be unavailable to all users, including administrators, between 7 p.m. and 7 a.m. regardless of their role or device. Which control enforces this?",c:["An account lockout threshold","A password expiration policy","A time-of-day restriction","A geofencing restriction policy"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Lockout disables an account after repeated failed logins. It is triggered by failed attempts and not by the clock. It would be right to slow password guessing.","Incorrect. Expiration forces users to change passwords after a set period, which does not affect when the system can be used. It would be right for a rotation policy.","Correct. Time-of-day restrictions limit when accounts can authenticate or access a system, regardless of role. The nightly blackout window is the clue.","Incorrect. Geofencing limits access based on physical or network location, not time. It would be right if access had to be limited to a country or office."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"Administrators have no standing admin rights. When one requests elevation for a defined task, the system grants the privilege for two hours, then removes it automatically. Which concept is this?",c:["A shared break-glass account","Password vaulting with scheduled rotation","Just-in-time privileged access","Standing membership in Domain Admins"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A break-glass account is a shared emergency account kept sealed for outages, not a time-boxed grant for routine tasks. It would be right for recovering when the identity system is unavailable.","Incorrect. Vaulting stores and rotates credentials but does not by itself provide time-limited elevation of the administrator's own account. It would be right for protecting service account passwords.","Correct. JIT access grants elevated rights only when needed and for a limited duration, removing standing privilege. The automatic expiry after two hours is the clue.","Incorrect. Standing Domain Admin membership is exactly the always-on privilege that the design eliminates. It would only be acceptable where a rigid workflow demands it."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"A privileged access management product keeps domain admin passwords, lets an engineer check one out for a session, records that session, and changes the password as soon as it is returned. Which feature does the check-out and automatic change describe?",c:["Federated single sign-on (SSO)","Privileged password vaulting","Access attestation campaign","Identity proofing at enrollment"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Federated SSO lets users authenticate once across trusted systems. It does not manage checkout or rotation of privileged passwords. It would be right for reducing repeated sign-ins.","Correct. Vaulting stores privileged credentials, controls checkout, rotates them after use and logs activity, matching the behavior described.","Incorrect. Attestation is a periodic review of who has which access. It would be right for a quarterly manager review.","Incorrect. Identity proofing verifies a person's real-world identity before an account is issued. It would be right when onboarding new employees or customers."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"Every quarter, each manager receives a list of their employees' application entitlements and must confirm or revoke each item, so that unneeded access is removed. What is this process called?",c:["Access recertification review","Account provisioning workflow","Identity federation agreement","A password spraying attack"],a:0,e:["Correct. Recertification, also called attestation, is a periodic owner review that catches and removes excess access. The manager review of entitlements is the clue.","Incorrect. Provisioning is creating and assigning access during onboarding. It would be right for setting up a new hire's accounts.","Incorrect. Federation creates trust between identity systems so credentials are accepted across organizations. It does not review entitlements. It would be right for partner SSO.","Incorrect. Password spraying is an attack that tries a few common passwords across many accounts. It is not a governance process."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"Over eight years, an employee moved through four departments and still holds the access from every previous role, including the ability to approve payments. What is this problem called?",c:["A case of privilege escalation","A failure of separation of duties","A case of lateral movement","A case of privilege creep"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Privilege escalation is an attacker or user gaining higher rights than intended, usually by exploiting a flaw. Here the rights were legitimately granted over time.","Incorrect. Separation of duties is a control that splits critical tasks between people. The accumulated access here may violate it, but it names the control, not the problem.","Incorrect. Lateral movement is an attacker pivoting across systems after compromise. It would be right for describing activity inside a breached network.","Correct. Privilege creep is the accumulation of rights as people change roles without old access being removed. Regular recertification is the fix."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"When HR marks an employee as terminated, all of that person's directory, email and SaaS accounts are disabled within minutes without any ticket from IT. Which process is this?",c:["Periodic access attestation","Federated trust renewal process","Scheduled password rotation","Automated deprovisioning workflow"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Attestation is a scheduled review that finds excess access after the fact, not an immediate event-driven removal. It would be right for catching stale entitlements quarterly.","Incorrect. Renewing federated trust deals with certificates and metadata between identity providers, not with ending an employee's access. It would be right when a partner IdP certificate expires.","Incorrect. Rotating passwords changes credentials on a schedule but leaves the account active for the departed employee. It would be right for reducing the lifetime of shared secrets.","Correct. Deprovisioning triggered by an authoritative HR source removes access quickly and consistently and avoids orphaned accounts. The HR event and no ticket are the clues."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"Employees use the same password pattern, such as CompanyName123!, for email, the CRM and the VPN. Which action addresses the root problem MOST directly?",c:["Keep the same passwords but require MFA on the VPN and email only","Require all users to change their passwords every month on a schedule","Require unique passwords per system, stored in a password manager","Increase the minimum length of the password by two characters"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. MFA on two systems helps with those logins but leaves the CRM exposed and does not stop reuse. It would be right as an added layer rather than the fix.","Incorrect. Forced monthly changes tend to produce predictable increments of the same pattern, such as 124 or 125. Rotation is appropriate after a known compromise, not as the primary fix.","Correct. The weakness is reuse and predictability, and unique generated passwords remove both, so a breach of one system no longer exposes the others.","Incorrect. Two more characters of the same predictable pattern add little entropy and do nothing about reuse. Length helps when users choose truly random passphrases."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"A company is rewriting its password policy to follow current NIST SP 800-63B guidance. Which change should the policy include?",c:["Require complex composition rules and a mandatory change every 60 days","Allow long passphrases and screen new passwords against breach lists","Require answers to security questions for password resets","Limit passwords to 12 characters to ease memorization"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. NIST recommends against composition rules and forced periodic changes without evidence of compromise, as they lead to predictable passwords. It matches older policies.","Correct. NIST favors length, screening against compromised passwords, and no arbitrary periodic rotation. Allowing long passphrases and checking breach lists reflects that.","Incorrect. Knowledge-based security questions are discouraged because answers are guessable or discoverable. Stronger recovery options include out-of-band verification.","Incorrect. NIST says systems should support long passwords, at least 64 characters, and not impose a short maximum. A 12-character cap makes passwords weaker."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"An administrator must open a firewall rule so applications can query user and group objects from the directory using the standard unencrypted directory access protocol. Which port is it?",c:["TCP 389 from the applications to the directory servers","TCP 636 from the applications to the directory servers","TCP 88 from the applications to the directory servers","UDP 1812 from the applications to the directory servers"],a:0,e:["Correct. LDAP uses TCP 389 by default for directory queries. The unencrypted clue distinguishes it from the secured version.","Incorrect. TCP 636 is LDAPS, which is LDAP over TLS. It would be right if the stem required an encrypted directory connection.","Incorrect. TCP 88 is Kerberos for ticket-based authentication, not directory queries. It would be right for domain authentication traffic.","Incorrect. UDP 1812 is RADIUS authentication for network access. It would be right for 802.1X or VPN authentication, not directory lookups."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"During account creation for a new customer in a regulated bank, the bank checks a government ID document and matches it against a selfie before any credentials are issued. What is this step called?",c:["The identity proofing step","The identity federation step","The authorization decision step","The password recovery step"],a:0,e:["Correct. Identity proofing verifies that a person is who they claim to be before credentials are issued, using documents and biometric matching. The pre-issuance check is the clue.","Incorrect. Federation lets one provider's authentication be trusted by another. It would be right if the bank accepted a partner's login rather than checking an ID itself.","Incorrect. Authorization decides what an authenticated identity may do. It occurs after the identity is established. It would be right for assigning account privileges.","Incorrect. Password resetting restores access for an existing account and occurs later in the lifecycle. It would be right when a returning customer forgets credentials."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"A company is selecting a single sign-on platform for 60 existing SaaS applications. Which factor is MOST important in determining whether SSO will work across all of them?",c:["Whether each vendor offers a mobile client for its app","Whether the applications share the same data center","Whether the vendors use the same support ticket system","Whether each app supports SAML or OIDC federation"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Mobile client availability affects user experience but not whether the app can accept federated authentication. It would matter in a mobile device strategy.","Incorrect. Hosting location may affect latency or residency but is unrelated to authentication standards. It would matter for data sovereignty.","Incorrect. Support systems affect operations and have no bearing on federated login. They would matter when planning vendor management.","Correct. SSO depends on the applications and the IdP speaking a common federation protocol such as SAML or OIDC, so protocol support determines integration feasibility."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"Five IT staff share a single account named admin to manage the file servers. Which is the PRIMARY security problem with this practice?",c:["Passwords for shared accounts cannot be hashed in the directory store","Shared accounts cannot be placed in any security group","Actions cannot be attributed to one person, so accountability is lost","Shared accounts cannot be audited by the operating system"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Shared account passwords are hashed like any other. The problem is not storage but attribution of actions.","Incorrect. A shared account can be placed in groups like any other. Group membership is not the issue; accountability is.","Correct. With a shared credential the logs show only admin, so no person can be held accountable and non-repudiation is lost. Named accounts and a PAM tool restore attribution.","Incorrect. Operating systems do log events from shared accounts, but the logs cannot identify which person acted. That is why attribution is lost, not auditing as such."]},
{o:"4.6",q:"A secure facility requires a biometric authenticator, one that falls under something you are. Which of the following qualifies?",c:["A hardware security key device","A voiceprint recognition system","A numeric PIN entered at login","A GPS location check at login"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A hardware key is a physical object the user possesses. It would be right for a something-you-have requirement.","Correct. A voiceprint is a physiological or behavioral characteristic of the person, so it is a biometric inherence factor.","Incorrect. A PIN is a secret the user remembers, so it is knowledge. It would be right for a something-you-know requirement.","Incorrect. A GPS location check supports the somewhere-you-are factor. It would be right when access depends on where the device is located."]},

// ---------- 4.7 Automation and orchestration (14) ----------
{o:"4.7",q:"A SOC wants one platform that automatically enriches each alert with threat intelligence, runs a playbook of response steps across the firewall and EDR, and opens a ticket, with analysts approving only the risky steps. Which technology fits?",c:["SIEM with correlation rules and dashboards","SOAR with playbooks and tool integrations","NAC with posture assessment policies","DLP with endpoint and network sensors"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A SIEM aggregates and correlates logs to generate alerts and dashboards, but it is not designed to execute response actions across other tools. It would be right for detection and log analysis.","Correct. SOAR orchestrates multiple tools and automates response workflows through playbooks, with human approval gates where needed. Cross-tool actions are the clue.","Incorrect. NAC enforces device admission policy at connection time. It does not enrich alerts or run response playbooks. It would be right for quarantining a noncompliant laptop.","Incorrect. DLP detects and blocks sensitive data movement. It is not an orchestration layer for responding to alerts. It would be right for preventing data exfiltration."]},
{o:"4.7",q:"HR wants new hires to receive directory, email and application access automatically when the HR record is created, and to lose it automatically at termination. What is the MAIN security benefit of this automation?",c:["Consistent, timely access changes with fewer orphaned accounts","Elimination of the need for authentication on internal applications","Removal of the need for periodic access reviews by managers","Guaranteed protection against compromised administrator credentials"],a:0,e:["Correct. Automation applies the same rules each time and quickly removes access at offboarding, which reduces human error and orphaned accounts.","Incorrect. Users must still authenticate. Automation only changes how accounts and entitlements are created and removed.","Incorrect. Reviews are still needed to catch exceptions and drift from role-based rules. Automation reduces but does not replace attestation.","Incorrect. Automated lifecycle management does nothing to protect stolen credentials. It would be right to use MFA and PAM controls for that risk."]},
{o:"4.7",q:"The SIEM, EDR, ticketing system and firewall were purchased from four vendors. Which feature MOST enables the security team to exchange data between them and trigger actions in each automatically?",c:["Shared hardware rack space in one data center","A common logo and user interface theme","A single support contract with one reseller","Exposed APIs with documented authentication"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Physical proximity does not let software systems communicate or invoke actions. It might matter for latency or hosting costs.","Incorrect. Visual branding such as a logo or interface theme has no bearing on whether tools can exchange data and trigger actions. It would matter when considering user adoption or consistent look and feel across consoles.","Incorrect. A shared support contract simplifies purchasing but does not connect the tools. It would be relevant to vendor management.","Correct. APIs let tools exchange data and trigger actions programmatically, which underpins integration and orchestration across vendors."]},
{o:"4.7",q:"A company routes every enrichment, ticketing and containment action through one orchestration server. When that server crashes during an incident, none of those processes run. Which risk does this illustrate?",c:["Technical debt accumulation","A workforce multiplier effect","A single point of failure","Guardrail misconfiguration"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Technical debt is the future rework cost from shortcuts or poor design. The scenario describes an availability dependency, not accumulated shortcuts.","Incorrect. A workforce multiplier is a benefit of automation, where staff accomplish more. It is not a risk.","Correct. Concentrating many functions in one tool means one outage disrupts them all, which is a single point of failure. Fix this with redundancy and manual fallback procedures.","Incorrect. A guardrail misconfiguration would mean an automated limit is set wrongly and allows unsafe actions, but no such limit is involved here; the failure is an outage of the tool itself. It would be right if an automated control wrongly allowed a risky change."]},
{o:"4.7",q:"Before approving a large security automation project, an architect lists concerns. Which set matches the consideration and risk areas CompTIA associates with automation?",c:["Reduced consistency, slower response time, larger staffing needs and constant manual effort","Lower scalability, more human error, weaker baselines and unpredictable enforcement","Complexity, cost, single point of failure, technical debt and ongoing supportability","Less integration, no audit trail, higher reaction time and fewer enforceable standards"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. This list describes the problems that automation is meant to reduce, such as inconsistency and slow response. They are benefits being reversed, not risks of automation.","Incorrect. Automation generally improves scalability, reduces human error and strengthens baselines. This is a list of manual-process weaknesses.","Correct. These are the recognized considerations: automation adds complexity and cost, can concentrate failure, can accumulate technical debt and needs continued support.","Incorrect. Automation usually improves integration, creates audit trails and reduces reaction time. This list describes a manual environment."]},
{o:"4.7",q:"Over three years the SOC accumulated quick, undocumented scripts that only their author understands. Several break whenever a vendor changes an API, and fixing them now takes more effort than rewriting them. What is this called?",c:["Unauthorized shadow IT use","Accumulated technical debt","Configuration drift on hosts","Unchecked project scope creep"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Shadow IT is technology used without IT approval. These scripts were built by the security team, so the issue is quality and maintainability, not authorization.","Correct. Technical debt is the accumulated cost of rework caused by hasty, poorly designed or undocumented solutions.","Incorrect. Configuration drift is when systems diverge from their approved baseline over time. It would be right for servers that no longer match their standard build.","Incorrect. Scope creep is uncontrolled growth of a project's requirements. It would be right for a project that keeps expanding beyond its original plan."]},
{o:"4.7",q:"The SIEM generates a critical alert, and a script immediately creates a ticket and pages the on-call engineer with the alert details. What is the key benefit of this automation?",c:["Faster, consistent response with an audit trail","Elimination of false positives from the SIEM rules","Complete replacement of human on-call duties","Reduced volume of events collected from sources"],a:0,e:["Correct. Automated ticketing ensures every critical alert is routed the same way, quickly, and recorded for audit and metrics.","Incorrect. Ticket creation does not change detection quality. Tuning the correlation rules would reduce false positives.","Incorrect. The on-call engineer is still paged to act. Automation of routing does not remove human decision-making.","Incorrect. Event volume is determined by log sources and filtering. Creating tickets does not reduce what is collected."]},
{o:"4.7",q:"A cloud team wants a control that automatically blocks any deployment that would make a storage bucket publicly readable, regardless of who submits it. Which concept is this?",c:["An automated playbook sequence","A documented runbook procedure","A formal escalation procedure","An automated guardrail policy"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A playbook is a set of steps followed in response to an event. It would be right for a documented response when a public bucket is found.","Incorrect. A runbook is a documented procedure for performing an operational task, often partly automated. It describes how to do a task, not a preventive block.","Incorrect. Escalation passes an issue to higher or more specialized staff. It would be right when an analyst cannot resolve an incident.","Correct. A guardrail is an automated preventive control that keeps actions within policy, such as blocking public buckets. Automatic blocking regardless of submitter is the clue."]},
{o:"4.7",q:"The CISO says automation is a workforce multiplier for a five-person security team. What does she mean?",c:["The team can be reduced to a single analyst without losing coverage","Licensing costs fall because several tools are consolidated into one product","Analysts are cross-trained on every tool so any member can cover any role","Repetitive tasks are automated so analysts can focus on higher-value work"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. The idea is to amplify staff, not eliminate them. Judgment, oversight and incident decisions still require people. It would be right only for a scenario where staffing cuts were the stated goal, which the term does not describe.","Incorrect. Tool consolidation may reduce licensing cost, but a workforce multiplier concerns analyst capacity, not the number of products. It would be right in a vendor-consolidation business case.","Incorrect. Cross-training improves coverage and resilience, but the multiplier phrase refers to automation extending what the team can output. It would be right when discussing personnel redundancy.","Correct. Automation extends the capacity of a small team by offloading repeatable work, freeing humans for investigation and judgment."]},
{o:"4.7",q:"Which of these tasks is the BEST candidate for security automation?",c:["Deciding the organization's risk appetite and acceptable residual risk","Negotiating the liability terms of a managed security services contract","Enriching IOCs and resetting locked accounts after verification","Interviewing and selecting a new chief information security officer"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Risk appetite is a leadership decision based on business context and requires human judgment.","Incorrect. Contract negotiation involves legal, financial and relationship judgment that cannot be reduced to rules.","Correct. Repetitive, rule-based and well-defined tasks are ideal for automation because they are consistent and high volume.","Incorrect. Selecting an executive requires human evaluation of fit, trust and leadership judgment, which cannot be reduced to repeatable rules. Automation fits repetitive, well-defined tasks such as enrichment and account resets instead."]},
{o:"4.7",q:"A cloud provider hosts 5,000 new virtual machines a month. Which advantage does automated configuration enforcement give the security team as the environment scales?",c:["Each administrator can choose settings that fit local needs","Every new system receives the same approved baseline","Hardening can wait until after production go-live","Security reviews can be skipped for low-risk systems"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Allowing ad hoc settings is the opposite of consistent enforcement and produces inconsistent baselines.","Correct. Automation applies standard configurations consistently as the environment grows, preventing drift and gaps that manual builds would produce.","Incorrect. Delaying hardening until after go-live exposes new systems to attack during their most vulnerable period. Automated baselines are applied during provisioning, so hardening happens before production, not after.","Incorrect. Reviews are risk-based and not skipped because of automation. Automation supports them rather than replacing them."]},
{o:"4.7",q:"A manager asks what ongoing supportability means for the team's automation. Which statement is accurate?",c:["Scripts and integrations need owners, documentation and updates when systems change","Automation tools are self-maintaining once the initial playbooks have been tested","Supportability is a vendor duty for any purchased automation platform","Documentation is required for scripts that run with administrator privileges"],a:0,e:["Correct. Without owners, documentation and updates, automation breaks when APIs or systems change and turns into technical debt.","Incorrect. Automation does not maintain itself. Changes in integrated systems, APIs and credentials regularly break playbooks, so tested workflows still require updates. Self-maintaining tools would be a vendor claim, not a reliable assumption.","Incorrect. Even with purchased platforms the organization maintains its own integrations, playbooks and configuration, so supportability is shared and internal. Vendor-only support would apply to the platform code itself, not your workflows.","Incorrect. Any production automation needs documentation and an owner regardless of the privilege it runs with, since others must be able to troubleshoot it. Limiting documentation to privileged scripts leaves most workflows unsupportable."]},
{o:"4.7",q:"A script automatically enables MFA and adds each new account to the baseline security groups at creation time. Which benefit does this MOST directly illustrate?",c:["Consistent baseline enforcement","Introducing a single point of failure","Accumulating technical debt","Improving the vendor's support contract"],a:0,e:["Correct. Applying the same controls to every account without relying on manual steps is consistent baseline enforcement.","Incorrect. A single point of failure is a risk that arises from over-dependence on one tool. The scenario describes a benefit, not a failure.","Incorrect. Technical debt is a drawback that results from poor design. This well-defined script does the opposite.","Incorrect. A support contract is a procurement matter and is unrelated to the effect of a script that enables MFA and assigns baseline groups at account creation. It would be relevant when evaluating vendor service levels."]},
{o:"4.7",q:"An auditor asks about ticket creation in the SOAR playbooks. Which of these statements about ticket creation is NOT true?",c:["It improves communication and coordination between teams","It creates an audit trail of what was done and when","It makes it possible to track response and resolution times","It removes the need for human decisions during incidents"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. This statement is true. Tickets provide a shared record that teams use to coordinate, so it is not the answer to a NOT question.","Incorrect. This statement is true. Each ticket records actions and timestamps, creating an audit trail.","Incorrect. This statement is true. Ticket timestamps support metrics such as time to respond and resolve.","Correct. This statement is false: tickets organize and route work, but people still decide how to respond to incidents. The question asks for the false statement."]},

// ---------- 4.8 Incident response (18) ----------
{o:"4.8",q:"Which sequence lists the incident response process in the order used by CompTIA?",c:["Preparation, analysis, detection, eradication, containment, recovery, lessons learned","Detection, preparation, containment, analysis, recovery, eradication, lessons learned","Preparation, detection, analysis, containment, eradication, recovery, lessons learned","Preparation, detection, containment, analysis, recovery, eradication, lessons learned"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Detection precedes analysis, because an event must be noticed before it is analyzed. Containment also comes before eradication.","Incorrect. Preparation must come first, before any incident, and analysis precedes containment so the scope is known. Eradication comes before recovery.","Correct. The order is preparation, detection, analysis, containment, eradication, recovery, then lessons learned. Each phase depends on the previous one: you cannot analyze before detecting or restore before the threat is removed.","Incorrect. Analysis comes before containment so responders understand what to contain, and eradication comes before recovery so systems are not restored while the threat persists."]},
{o:"4.8",q:"An analyst confirms ransomware is encrypting two file servers, and the investigation has identified the affected hosts. According to the process, what should the team do NEXT?",c:["Eradicate the malware by wiping and reimaging the servers","Contain the incident by isolating the affected servers","Recover operations by restoring the servers from last night's backup","Conduct a lessons-learned review with all stakeholders"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Eradication comes after containment, and wiping now would also destroy volatile evidence. It would be right once the spread is stopped and evidence is collected.","Correct. With the incident confirmed and scoped, containment limits further damage, for example by isolating hosts, while preserving evidence.","Incorrect. Restoring from backup while the malware may still be active or the servers are still connected risks immediate reinfection. Recovery follows eradication, and it would be right after the threat is removed.","Incorrect. Lessons learned is the final phase and takes place after the incident is fully resolved. It would be right once the servers are restored and the team reviews what happened."]},
{o:"4.8",q:"Before any incident occurs, a company writes response playbooks, trains staff, sets up communication channels and deploys logging and forensic tools. Which phase of incident response is this?",c:["The detection phase of the IR process","The preparation phase of the IR process","The recovery phase of the IR process","The lessons learned phase of the IR process"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Detection is identifying that an event is occurring, using the tools deployed in preparation. It happens during an incident.","Correct. Preparation builds the people, process and tooling needed to respond effectively before an incident takes place.","Incorrect. Recovery restores systems to normal after the threat is removed. It happens after an incident.","Incorrect. Lessons learned reviews a completed incident to improve processes. It happens after the incident, even though its outputs feed back into preparation."]},
{o:"4.8",q:"After the malware has been removed and the vulnerable service patched, the team rebuilds servers from known-good images, restores data from clean backups and monitors the systems closely. Which phase is this?",c:["The recovery phase of the IR process","The containment phase of the IR process","The eradication phase of the IR process","The analysis phase of the IR process"],a:0,e:["Correct. Recovery returns systems to normal operation, with heightened monitoring to catch reinfection. The restoring and monitoring steps are the clue.","Incorrect. Containment limits the spread of an incident, for example by isolation. It occurs earlier.","Incorrect. Eradication is removing malware, closing the vulnerability and removing attacker persistence. Those steps were already completed in this scenario.","Incorrect. Analysis determines scope and cause. It occurs before containment and is not about restoring services."]},
{o:"4.8",q:"The incident team deletes the attacker's scheduled tasks and backdoor accounts, removes the malware, and applies the patch for the exploited flaw. Which phase is this?",c:["The recovery phase of the IR process","The containment phase of the IR process","The detection phase of the IR process","The eradication phase of the IR process"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Recovery restores services and data once the threat has been removed. Restoring is not described here.","Incorrect. Containment limits spread, for example by isolating or blocking, and does not remove persistence mechanisms.","Incorrect. Detection is identification of the incident. It is already finished by the time persistence is being removed.","Correct. Eradication removes the attacker's foothold and the root cause so the threat cannot return."]},
{o:"4.8",q:"Two weeks after a breach was resolved, leadership holds a blameless meeting to review the timeline, what worked and what failed, and to produce action items. What is the PRIMARY goal of this meeting?",c:["Determine which employee should receive formal discipline","Collect and preserve forensic evidence from affected hosts","Improve processes and controls for future incidents","Notify customers and regulators about the breach details"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Assigning discipline is not the goal and discourages candid reporting. HR processes handle that separately if needed.","Incorrect. Evidence collection happens during the incident, particularly in containment and analysis, not two weeks after resolution.","Correct. The lessons-learned phase feeds improvements back into preparation and controls. A blameless review is how teams get honest information.","Incorrect. Notifications follow legal and communication plans and typically occur sooner. It is a separate process from a review meeting."]},
{o:"4.8",q:"Which activity BEST fits the analysis phase of incident response?",c:["Configuring a new firewall and SIEM before any events occur","Restoring corrupted databases from verified backups","Determining scope, affected systems and the entry point","Updating the acceptable use policy after the incident"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Deploying tools before events occur is preparation. It would be right in the stem if no incident were in progress.","Incorrect. Restoring corrupted databases is part of recovery, which follows eradication. It would be the right answer if the stem described returning systems to service after the threat was removed.","Correct. Analysis establishes what happened, how far it spread and how the attacker got in, so containment can be targeted.","Incorrect. Updating the acceptable use policy after an incident is an improvement activity from the lessons-learned phase. It would be right if the stem described post-incident review actions."]},
{o:"4.8",q:"Company executives meet around a table and walk through a ransomware scenario, discussing who decides whether to pay, who calls legal counsel and who speaks to the press. No systems are touched. What type of exercise is this?",c:["A live functional simulation","A tabletop exercise session","A scoped penetration test","A formal post-incident review"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A live simulation involves hands-on activity, such as responders working actual tools or systems. It would be right if teams carried out technical response steps.","Correct. A tabletop is a discussion-based exercise that tests plans and decision-making without touching production systems.","Incorrect. A penetration test actively attacks systems to find weaknesses. Here, nothing is tested technically.","Incorrect. A post-incident review analyzes a real incident that has happened. This is a hypothetical scenario."]},
{o:"4.8",q:"A forensic examiner logs every person who touched a hard drive, with date, time and purpose, from the seizure through lab analysis. Why is this documentation needed?",c:["To maintain chain of custody for admissibility","To prove which risk was assigned the highest ALE","To demonstrate the drive's data was properly encrypted","To justify the retention period for the legal department"],a:0,e:["Correct. Chain of custody documents continuous control and handling, showing the evidence was not altered or tampered with.","Incorrect. ALE is a risk assessment metric and has nothing to do with handling of evidence.","Incorrect. Handling logs do not show encryption status. A hash would show integrity and encryption would be shown by configuration records.","Incorrect. Retention periods are set by policy and law. The custody log does not establish them."]},
{o:"4.8",q:"Counsel tells IT that a lawsuit is likely and instructs the team to preserve all relevant emails and files and to suspend automatic deletion for those custodians. What is this instruction?",c:["An e-discovery production order","A chain of custody log entry","A data retention schedule","A litigation legal hold order"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. E-discovery is the later process of identifying, collecting and producing electronic records in litigation. The hold comes first.","Incorrect. Chain of custody documents who handled evidence. It does not itself require suspending deletion.","Incorrect. A retention schedule defines how long data is kept before deletion. A legal hold overrides it.","Correct. A legal hold requires preserving potentially relevant data and suspending deletion because litigation is anticipated."]},
{o:"4.8",q:"In litigation, opposing counsel requests specific emails, chat messages and files from the company. The company must identify, collect and produce them in a defensible way. What is this process?",c:["A legal hold preservation notice","A proactive threat hunting effort","A root cause analysis exercise","The e-discovery production process"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A legal hold preserves data to prevent deletion. It is an earlier step and does not include producing it to the other party.","Incorrect. Threat hunting proactively searches for undetected attackers. It is a security operations activity, not a legal one.","Incorrect. Root cause analysis finds why an incident occurred. It is not a process for producing records.","Correct. E-discovery is the process of identifying, collecting and producing electronically stored information for legal proceedings."]},
{o:"4.8",q:"An investigator must collect evidence from a running compromised server. According to the order of volatility, in what order should data be collected?",c:["Disk, swap file, RAM, CPU cache and registers, archived backups","Archived backups, disk, swap file, RAM, CPU cache and registers","CPU cache and registers, RAM, swap file, disk, archived backups","RAM, disk, CPU cache and registers, archived backups, swap file"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. This puts disk before RAM and cache. Memory contents vanish at power loss or change quickly, so they must be captured first.","Incorrect. This is almost the reverse. Archived media is the least volatile and should be collected last.","Correct. The most volatile data is collected first because it is lost soonest: CPU data, RAM, swap, disk, then archives.","Incorrect. This places swap and CPU data out of order. Cache and registers change the fastest and come before RAM, and swap is more volatile than disk."]},
{o:"4.8",q:"Immediately after imaging a suspect laptop's drive, the examiner computes a SHA-256 hash of the image and records it, then works only from copies. Why?",c:["To compress the image so it fits on the evidence server","To prove the image has not changed since acquisition","To encrypt the image so only the examiner can open it","To identify which user account last modified the files"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Hashing produces a fixed-length digest and does not compress the data. Compression would be a separate step.","Correct. Matching hash values show that the evidence is unchanged, which supports integrity and admissibility.","Incorrect. Hashing is not encryption and does not restrict access. Disk encryption or encrypted evidence storage would do that.","Incorrect. A hash of the whole image says nothing about who changed individual files. File metadata and logs would show that."]},
{o:"4.8",q:"An analyst forms a hypothesis that an attacker is using stolen credentials to move laterally, and searches authentication logs and EDR data for matching patterns even though no alert has fired. What is this activity?",c:["A threat hunting activity","A tabletop planning exercise","A credentialed vulnerability scan","A digital forensics acquisition"],a:0,e:["Correct. Threat hunting is the proactive, hypothesis-driven search for undetected adversary activity, assuming a breach may already exist.","Incorrect. A tabletop is a discussion-based planning exercise and involves no searching of authentication logs or EDR data. It would be right if executives were rehearsing decisions around a table.","Incorrect. A vulnerability scan identifies weaknesses in systems, not attacker behavior hidden in logs. It would be right if the goal were finding unpatched software rather than hunting for lateral movement.","Incorrect. Forensic acquisition preserves copies of evidence from a known incident. This work is searching for an unknown one."]},
{o:"4.8",q:"After a web server is breached, the team wants to know why the attacker could get in, such as an unpatched library, a misconfigured firewall rule or a weak admin password, so the underlying issue can be fixed permanently. What is this called?",c:["Root cause analysis of the breach","Containment of the web server","Recovery testing of the server","Evidence acquisition from the server"],a:0,e:["Correct. Root cause analysis finds the underlying reason for an incident so the cause rather than the symptom is corrected.","Incorrect. Containment limits damage. It would be right for isolating the server to stop the attack spreading.","Incorrect. Recovery testing verifies that restored systems operate correctly. It does not find why the breach happened.","Incorrect. Evidence acquisition collects and preserves data for investigation or court. It does not itself determine the underlying cause."]},
{o:"4.8",q:"A manager questions why the company spends time training all responders on the incident response plan every year. What is the BEST justification?",c:["Training satisfies the annual compliance audit without changing response times","Training allows responders to improvise instead of following written procedures","Training lowers cyber insurance premiums for the following policy year","Responders act quickly and correctly under pressure during an incident"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Compliance may require training, but the stated benefit asked for is operational readiness during an incident. It would be right if the question asked how to meet an audit requirement.","Incorrect. Training reinforces the written plan so responders follow consistent procedures, rather than improvising. Improvisation would be appropriate only for situations the plan does not cover.","Incorrect. Insurers may consider security maturity, but premium reduction is not the operational reason to train responders. It would be right in a cost-justification discussion with an insurer.","Correct. Practiced responders follow procedures, communicate clearly and reduce delays and mistakes during real incidents."]},
{o:"4.8",q:"An alert has just fired on the SIEM. Which incident response activity comes immediately next, determining whether the alert represents a real incident and how serious it is?",c:["Eradication of the suspected threat from the host","Lessons-learned review with senior management","Detection and analysis through alert triage","Preparation of new playbooks for the alert type"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Eradication follows containment, and acting before confirming a true positive wastes effort or disrupts systems. It would be right once the incident is scoped and contained.","Incorrect. Lessons learned occurs after resolution. It cannot be next when the alert is fresh.","Correct. The first step after an alert is to validate and triage it to decide whether it is a genuine incident and to understand its severity.","Incorrect. Writing new playbooks is a preparation activity done before incidents or during improvement, not as a response to this alert."]},
{o:"4.8",q:"The CISO asks what the incident response plan itself MUST contain to be usable at 2 a.m. during a crisis. Which list is the BEST answer?",c:["Vulnerability scan schedules, patch windows and change freeze calendars","Roles and responsibilities, communication and escalation paths, and procedures","Business impact analysis results, recovery objectives and backup schedules","Asset inventory, network diagrams and firewall rule exports for each site"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. These belong to vulnerability and change management processes. They support response but do not define who acts or how. They would be right in a patch management plan.","Correct. A plan must say who does what, how to communicate and escalate, and what steps to follow so responders can act quickly.","Incorrect. BIA outputs and recovery objectives belong in the business continuity and disaster recovery plans; the IR plan may reference them but is built around roles and procedures. They would be right in a DR plan.","Incorrect. Inventories and diagrams are valuable reference attachments, but a plan without roles, contacts or procedures cannot direct a response. They would be right as appendices."]},

// ---------- 4.9 Data sources for investigations (16) ----------
{o:"4.9",q:"An investigator needs to determine which internal host contacted a known command-and-control IP last Tuesday and roughly how many bytes were sent, but full payload content is not required. Which data source is BEST?",c:["Application logs from the public web server","NetFlow records from the core router","Metadata embedded in a recovered Word file","Vulnerability scan results from the last quarter"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A web server's logs show requests received by that server, not outbound connections from an internal workstation. They would be right for investigating attacks against the web application.","Correct. Flow records show source and destination addresses, ports, times and byte counts, which answers who talked to the C2 address and how much was sent. No payload is needed, which fits flow data.","Incorrect. File metadata describes a document, such as author and timestamps, and says nothing about network connections. It would be right for establishing who created a file.","Incorrect. Scan results list weaknesses on systems at scan time and do not record traffic. They would be right for determining whether a vulnerability existed before an exploit."]},
{o:"4.9",q:"An investigator wants to know which accounts logged on successfully and unsuccessfully to a Windows server, and when privileges were assigned or an account was changed. Which source is BEST?",c:["The Windows Security event log on the server","NetFlow records from the nearest switch","A packet capture of the server's network segment","Perimeter firewall allow and deny logs"],a:0,e:["Correct. The OS security log records authentication events, such as 4624 and 4625, as well as privilege use and account changes on that host.","Incorrect. NetFlow summarizes conversations between IP addresses and does not record which account logged on. It would be right for measuring traffic volume between hosts.","Incorrect. A packet capture may show encrypted authentication traffic but not a reliable record of account events. It would be right for examining the content of unencrypted sessions.","Incorrect. Firewall logs show connection decisions at the network edge and not logon results inside the server. They would be right for seeing whether external traffic was permitted."]},
{o:"4.9",q:"An analyst must reconstruct the exact request headers and payload of a suspicious unencrypted HTTP session that exfiltrated a file. Which data source is BEST?",c:["NetFlow summaries of the connection","Windows event logs from the client","A SIEM dashboard showing top talkers","Full packet capture of the session"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. NetFlow records metadata such as addresses, ports and byte counts, with no payload. It would be right when only the existence and volume of the connection matter.","Incorrect. Event logs record host events such as logons and service changes, not the contents of network sessions. They would be right for tracing account activity.","Incorrect. A dashboard is a summarized view for spotting patterns and does not contain the original traffic. It would be right for identifying which hosts to investigate first.","Correct. Full packet capture records complete packets, including headers and payload, so the session can be reconstructed. The need for content decides it."]},
{o:"4.9",q:"A forensic examiner opens a recovered PDF to find out who created it and when it was last modified. What type of data is the examiner reading?",c:["Entries in the document server's access control list","The sender and recipient fields from the email header","The author and timestamp fields stored in the file","Rules matched in the perimeter firewall's connection log"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. An ACL shows who is permitted to access the file, not who authored it or when it was modified. It would be right when investigating permissions.","Incorrect. Email headers describe how a message traveled and who sent it, not the document attached to it. They would be right for tracing a phishing email.","Correct. Metadata embedded in files commonly includes creator, creation and modification dates, size and sometimes software or location details.","Incorrect. Firewall logs record connections allowed or denied, not the contents or properties of a document. They would be right for analyzing network traffic decisions."]},
{o:"4.9",q:"During a phishing investigation, an analyst reads the full header of a suspicious email to see how it reached the mailbox. What can the header reveal?",c:["The recipient's password hash and last interactive logon time","The sender's physical street address and the device serial number","The relay servers it passed through and SPF, DKIM and DMARC results","The private decryption keys protecting the recipient's mailbox"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Headers do not contain credential data. Password hashes and logon times are held in directory and server logs.","Incorrect. Headers expose IP addresses and hostnames of servers, not a physical address or hardware serial number.","Correct. Headers contain the Received chain showing relays and authentication results, which helps determine whether the message was spoofed.","Incorrect. Mailbox encryption keys are not placed in message headers. Key material is protected separately by the mail system or the user's client."]},
{o:"4.9",q:"Which log source would MOST directly confirm that a laptop's antimalware agent detected and quarantined a malicious file this morning?",c:["Intrusion prevention system logs from the network sensor","Endpoint protection logs on the laptop and console","DNS server query logs from the corporate resolver","Switch port logs showing link state and MAC changes"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. An IPS sees network traffic and records detections on the wire, but not what happened on the laptop afterward. It would be right for confirming an attack was blocked in transit.","Correct. Endpoint protection logs record detections and the actions taken, such as quarantine, for that specific host.","Incorrect. DNS logs show which names the laptop resolved, not whether malware was quarantined. They would be right for identifying domains contacted.","Incorrect. Switch logs show port events such as link up or down. They would be right for tracing a device being unplugged."]},
{o:"4.9",q:"An infected host is suspected of beaconing to a command-and-control server. The investigator wants the list of domain names it resolved in the minutes before the first connection. Which source is BEST?",c:["DNS query logs from the internal resolver","Application logs from the host's web browser","File metadata from the dropped executable","Vulnerability scan results for the host"],a:0,e:["Correct. Resolver logs record which client asked for which domain and when, and that shows the C2 lookups preceding the connection.","Incorrect. Application logs record events within one application and would not cover every resolution by all processes on the host. They would be right for debugging a specific app.","Incorrect. Metadata about the dropped file might show a compile time or author but not the names the host looked up. It would be right for attributing a malware sample.","Incorrect. A scan shows the host's exposed weaknesses at scan time, not its lookups. It would be right for finding the vulnerability exploited."]},
{o:"4.9",q:"A developer reports that a web application is returning database errors, and the security team suspects SQL injection. Which log source would show the application's own error messages and user actions?",c:["NetFlow data from the load balancer","Perimeter firewall connection logs","Operating system boot logs on the server","The web application's own logs"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. NetFlow shows connection metadata and volumes but not application errors or parameters. It would be right for spotting unusual traffic volumes.","Incorrect. Firewall logs show allowed and denied connections at the perimeter and not what happened within the application. They would be right for confirming whether a source IP reached the server.","Incorrect. Boot logs describe operating system startup events. They would be right for diagnosing service failures at boot.","Correct. Application logs capture errors, requests and user actions inside the application, so they show database errors and injected input."]},
{o:"4.9",q:"After a breach through an unpatched server, an auditor asks which source would show whether the organization KNEW about the exposed flaw before the attack. Which source is MOST useful?",c:["Email headers from the attacker's original phishing message","Metadata from files copied during the breach","Print server logs from the nearby office","Historical vulnerability scan reports for the server"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Email headers describe the path of a message, not the scanning history of a server. They would be right for investigating how a phish was delivered.","Incorrect. Metadata from stolen files shows properties of the files, not the vulnerability status. It would be right for identifying who authored the exposed documents.","Incorrect. Printer logs record print jobs and are unrelated to a server vulnerability. They would be right for investigating printing of sensitive documents.","Correct. Earlier scan reports show whether the flaw was identified and when, which establishes prior knowledge and whether remediation lagged."]},
{o:"4.9",q:"A SIEM dashboard shows a sudden spike in failed logins across several servers within an hour. What is the dashboard's role in the investigation?",c:["It serves as the preserved original evidence presented in court","It blocks the offending source addresses at the firewall","It highlights a pattern so analysts can pivot to raw logs","It resets the passwords of the targeted accounts automatically"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A dashboard is a derived view. The preserved raw logs with their integrity protections are the evidence.","Incorrect. A dashboard displays information and does not enforce controls. Blocking would be done by a firewall or IPS, possibly triggered by a SOAR playbook.","Correct. Dashboards summarize and visualize data to point analysts to anomalies, who then drill down into the raw events for details.","Incorrect. Resetting passwords is an identity management action. A dashboard only shows data unless it is connected to an orchestration workflow."]},
{o:"4.9",q:"An analyst needs to determine whether a specific inbound connection from an external IP was allowed through the network boundary or blocked, and which rule made the decision. Which log type is BEST?",c:["Application server access logs","Network firewall rule logs","Operating system security logs","Embedded file metadata fields"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Application logs only show what reached the application, so a blocked connection would never appear. They would be right for application-level events.","Correct. Firewall logs record permitted and denied connections, with the rule that matched, at the network boundary.","Incorrect. OS security logs record host events such as logons, not boundary decisions. They would be right for tracing account activity on a server.","Incorrect. File metadata describes documents and has no information about network rules. It would be right for tracing file authorship."]},
{o:"4.9",q:"An IPS alert is being reviewed by an analyst deciding whether the attack succeeded. Which details in the IPS log are MOST useful for the investigation?",c:["Signature name, source and destination, time and action taken","Sensor firmware version, uptime and CPU utilization history","License tier, signature subscription expiry and update schedule","Rack location, interface cabling and port speed of the sensor"],a:0,e:["Correct. These fields show what was detected, between which hosts, when, and whether the traffic was blocked or only alerted, which is what an analyst needs to judge impact.","Incorrect. Device health telemetry helps operations staff keep the sensor running but says nothing about what traffic was detected. It would be right when troubleshooting sensor performance.","Incorrect. Licensing and subscription data show whether signatures are current but not what the sensor saw. It would be right when auditing the product's coverage.","Incorrect. Physical and interface details help install or troubleshoot the sensor but do not describe a detected attack. It would be right during a hardware change."]},
{o:"4.9",q:"An investigator is correlating firewall, server and EDR logs, but events from different systems appear to occur out of order. Why must timestamps be synchronized across the log sources?",c:["So events across systems form one accurate timeline","So log storage requires less disk space after compression","So log files are automatically encrypted in transit","So malware is prevented from altering log entries"],a:0,e:["Correct. Consistent time sources, such as NTP, allow analysts to sequence events across devices, which is essential in correlation and in presenting a defensible timeline.","Incorrect. Time synchronization does not change storage requirements. Compression and retention settings control disk use.","Incorrect. Encryption of logs in transit is provided by protocols like TLS, not by synchronizing clocks.","Incorrect. Preventing tampering requires integrity controls, such as write-once storage or hashing. Synchronized time only makes the sequence reliable."]},
{o:"4.9",q:"A suspect photo was recovered from a seized phone. The examiner extracts the EXIF fields to learn the camera model, capture time and GPS coordinates. Which data source is being used?",c:["NetFlow traffic summary records","Network firewall connection logs","Vulnerability scan result reports","Embedded file metadata fields"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. NetFlow is network traffic summary data and is not stored inside image files. It would be right for tracing network connections.","Incorrect. Firewall logs record connection decisions at the network boundary, not photo properties. They would be right for seeing whether traffic was allowed.","Incorrect. A vulnerability scan reports weaknesses on systems. It would be right for assessing exposure, not for analyzing a photo.","Correct. EXIF is metadata embedded in image files and can include the device, timestamp and location."]},
{o:"4.9",q:"An insider is suspected of copying 3 GB of customer files to a USB drive on a laptop that was never connected to the VPN that day. Which source would BEST show this activity?",c:["NetFlow records from the internet edge router","DNS logs from the corporate resolver","Endpoint agent and DLP logs from the laptop","Email headers from the user's mailbox"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Flow records at the edge router only see traffic crossing the network, and a copy to a local USB drive generates none. They would be right for large uploads to an external site.","Incorrect. DNS logs show name lookups and a USB copy does not involve DNS. They would be right for tracing domains contacted.","Correct. Endpoint agents and DLP record device connections and file copies on the host itself, including writes to removable media, even when the laptop is offline.","Incorrect. Email headers show the path of messages. A file copy to a USB drive involves no email. They would be right for investigating phishing or mail-based exfiltration."]},
{o:"4.9",q:"A new analyst asks what distinguishes full packet captures from flow data when planning storage for an investigation platform. Which statement is accurate?",c:["Flow records hold complete payloads and need far more storage than packet captures","Packet captures hold full content but need far more storage than flow summaries","Packet captures and flow records contain identical information in different file formats","Packet captures hold only addresses and byte counts, while flows hold the payloads"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. This reverses the relationship. Flow records contain no payloads and are much smaller than captures.","Correct. PCAP preserves complete packets, including payloads, so it is rich and storage-heavy, while flow data summarizes conversations and is lightweight to keep for long periods.","Incorrect. The two sources differ greatly in detail and size. Flows are summaries, while captures contain the packet contents.","Incorrect. This swaps the descriptions. Addresses and byte counts are characteristic of flow data, and payload is only in packet captures."]}
]);
